---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B2: Describe how memory mapped files integrate into your virtual
>> memory subsystem.  Explain how the page fault and eviction
>> processes differ between swap pages and other pages.

We use the same frame table and page table structure as before. When a page fault happens for a mmapped file, the page 
is loaded from the file into a frame and the frame gets mapped into the process's address space. The page gets marked with 
its current status IN_MEMORY and its source as SOURCE_MMAP, meaning a memory-mapped file-backed page. This distinction is 
necessary because of the differing processes for frame evictions between traditional swap pages and mmapped pages. Assuming the 
page is dirty, swap pages get written to the swap space on disk with a status IN_SWAP (eventually getting updated to IN_FILESYS 
when written back), while mmapped pages immediately get written back to their file's location on disk with an IN_FILESYS staus. 
No writing action needs to take place with a clean page. The page fault handling process is the same for written-back swap pages 
and evicted mmapped pages, since they both have an IN_FILESYS status. The only difference is that an evicted swap pages will be 
loaded directly from swap if they have not yet been written back (marked with an IN_SWAP status).

>> B3: Explain how you determine whether a new file mapping overlaps
>> any existing segment.

We check the new mapping's virtual address range to see if it overlaps other mmapped files or other segments existing in the process's 
address space. We also use our SPT to track the mapped regions. This makes sure that our new mapping doesn't overwrite valid pages.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B4: Mappings created with "mmap" have similar semantics to those of
>> data demand-paged from executables, except that "mmap" mappings are
>> written back to their original files, not to swap.  This implies
>> that much of their implementation can be shared.  Explain why your
>> implementation either does or does not share much of the code for
>> the two situations.

We share a lot of code between mmap and executable pages. We use the same frame allocation and eviction policies as well as the same 
page table and frame table set-up. The main difference is the way that changes are written back. We write back mmaped pages to the source 
files, and executable pages to swap space (where they may be written back to their file on disk later). The eviction process is very similar 
between these two situations. We handle mmapped file write-back in a SOURCE_MMAP control flow in frame.c's frame_evict() that provides an 
alternate path to the default write-to-swap (assuming the page is dirty). In the page fault handler's load_page, no additional code was required 
to handle mmapped pages, because upon eviction they are marked with an IN_FILESYS status and will be handled like swap pages that were written back.

